{"language": "en", "duration": 96.8399375, "text": "Quick test. Answer without thinking. $100 today or $110 in a month. Whatever you chose, what comes next may surprise you. Waiting a month to earn $10 is a 10% return in one month. Repeated every month, that would be over 200% a year. No bank offers you that. Second question. $100 in 12 months or $110 in 13 months. Now most people pick the $110, yet it's exactly the same weight. One month for $10 more. Only the word now has disappeared. This reflex is called present bias. As soon as a reward is immediate, it weighs much more in your brain than the same reward later. Economists have observed it since the 1980s. And it gets expensive. It's what pushes saving off until tomorrow. What makes us buy now and pay later? What empties an account for a whim? Today's you is negotiating against the you of 30 years from now. And it's always today's you who is in the room. So how do you get around it? First, automate. A transfer to savings on payday before the present gets a vote. Then decide in advance. Commit to saving part of your next raise. Finally, give that money a purpose. Travel, security, freedom. A concrete future weighs more than a vague one. So what did you choose at the start? Write $100 or $110 in the comments. And tell us if you changed your mind on the second question.", "words": [{"word": "Quick", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.38}, {"word": "test.", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.7}, {"word": "Answer", "start": 1.08, "end": 1.42}, {"word": "without", "start": 1.42, "end": 1.74}, {"word": "thinking:", "start": 1.74, "end": 2.1}, {"word": "a", "start": 2.58, "end": 2.76}, {"word": "hundred", "start": 2.76, "end": 2.94}, {"word": "dollars", "start": 2.94, "end": 3.12}, {"word": "today,", "start": 3.12, "end": 3.78}, {"word": "or", "start": 3.78, "end": 4.48}, {"word": "a", "start": 4.48, "end": 4.65}, {"word": "hundred", "start": 4.65, "end": 4.82}, {"word": "and", "start": 4.82, "end": 5.0}, {"word": "ten", "start": 5.0, "end": 